  "prompt_text": "This schematic summarizes how tumors promote both thrombosis and abnormal platelet biology. A mass of tumor cells sits beside a blood vessel and releases several prothrombotic signals, including ADP, IgG, tissue factor, soluble tumor-derived factors, and exosomes. These signals stimulate thrombin generation and platelet activation, while tissue factor also drives conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin. The result is a clot-like network of activated platelets and fibrin, illustrating cancer-associated thrombosis.\n\nAt the same time, the figure shows tumors increasing platelet production. Tumor-derived IL-6 acts on the liver, which then produces TPO; this signal reaches the bone marrow and promotes megakaryocyte maturation and pro-platelet formation, leading to more circulating resting platelets. Thus, cancer can raise platelet count through a liver–bone marrow axis.\n\nThe diagram also emphasizes qualitative changes in platelets. Resting platelets encounter tumor-derived factors and exosomes in the circulation and undergo altered protein uptake, RNA uptake, and RNA splicing, indicating that tumors can reprogram platelet contents as well as abundance. Overall, the image links cancer to a triad of platelet activation, clot formation, and increased, molecularly altered platelets.",
